## Task

1. Write every `files[]` entry per its `content_outline`, following the
   type conventions in `{{ input.__task.source_folder }}/../../references/`.
   These reads and writes are independent; make the calls in parallel.
2. SKILL.md for `workflow` and `flow`: render the scaffold, do not
   hand-compose it:
   ```bash
   SIFU_TPL={{ input.__task.source_folder }}/../../templates/skill
   ~/.kiro/skills/home/template/scripts/render.sh \
       --template "$SIFU_TPL/<type>.md.j2" \
       --include-dir "$SIFU_TPL" \
       --include-dir ~/.kiro/skills/home/loomv2/templates \
       --json-vars /tmp/{{ input.design.frontmatter.name }}-skill-vars.json \
       > '{{ input.skill_dir }}/SKILL.md'
   ```
   The template header lists its variables. A non-zero exit is a render
   failure that leaves SKILL.md truncated: fix the vars and re-run; after 3
   failures, `$LOOM runtime fail` with the renderer's stderr.
3. Workflow:
   ```bash
   LOOM={{ input.__loom.runtime }}
   SIFU={{ input.__task.source_folder }}/../../scripts/sifu.sh
   ```
   Create task folders with `$LOOM task new <name> --loom-root <root>`,
   write `io.yaml` and the body file, then finish the scaffold:
   ```bash
   $SIFU loom-finalize '{{ input.skill_dir }}'
   ```
   It regenerates `io_types.py`, makes every `tool.sh` executable, re-pins
   and validates every graph. Surface: exit 0. Intent: every design task
   and io contract is present and wired as approved. Fix and re-run until
   exit 0; after 3 failed attempts, `$LOOM runtime fail` with the last
   error.
4. Script packages: `uv lock` in each `pyproject.toml` folder.

